<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Way</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Middle Path, Majjhimāpaṭipadā</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle Way, or Middle Path, is a Buddhist term for two aspects of the Buddha's teaching. The first is a spiritual practice that avoids both extreme asceticism and sensual indulgence, and is defined as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a> leading to awakening. The Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, which Buddhist tradition regards as the Buddha's first teaching after his awakening, presents it this way, addressing five ascetics. The second is teaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> &quot;by the middle&quot;, which avoids both eternalism and annihilationism in speaking of existence and personal identity. Y. Karunadasa notes it is not a compromise between the extremes.</p></div>
